> Derive the update and the first-order error claim

For a first-order system y'=f(t,y), take N fixed steps with nominal
h=(t1-t0)/N. Forward Euler uses

\[
y_{n+1}=y_n+h f(t_n,y_n).
\]

If the exact solution is twice continuously differentiable, Taylor's
formula over a step beginning at the exact state gives the one-step
defect y(t+h)-y(t)-h f(t,y(t))=O(h^2). Some texts divide this defect
by h when defining local truncation error, obtaining O(h); the
normalisation must be stated. Here "one-step defect" means the
unnormalised O(h^2) quantity.

Suppose f is Lipschitz in y with constant L on the relevant region,
and the solution's second derivative is bounded by M. With e[n]
the global state error and positive h, its norm obeys

\[
\|e_{n+1}\|\le(1+hL)\|e_n\|+\frac M2h^2.
\]

Starting with exact initial data and summing this geometric bound
gives ||e[N]|| <= M h ((1+hL)^N-1)/(2L) for L>0, bounded by
M h (exp(L T)-1)/(2L) on a fixed interval of length T. The L=0
limit is M T h/2. These assumptions give first-order global error,
not accuracy for an arbitrary black-box callback or an arbitrarily
large step. Finite arithmetic introduces additional rounding effects.

> Preserve the original coarse decay and error family

The original experiment integrates y'=-y from zero to four with
eight steps. Its endpoint is (1-1/2)^8=1/256, far below exp(-4).
For the refinement experiment over [0,1], with h=1/N,

\[
y_N=(1-h)^{1/h},\quad
\log y_N=-1-\frac h2+O(h^2),\quad
\frac{\exp(-1)-y_N}{h}\longrightarrow\frac1{2e}.
\]

The program measures this error directly and prints its ratio to the
preceding refinement. The displayed log-log error points come from
actual trajectories, not a prewritten power-law curve. For this decay
and 0<h<1, 1-h<exp(-h) explains undershooting at sample times; that
sign is not a general property of Euler errors on other equations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euler_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto f=[](double,const std::vector<double>& y){return std::vector<double>{-y[0]};};const auto coarse=dm::algorithms::euler_method_work(f,{1},0,4,8);
  std::print("Original coarse decay: final Euler {}; exact {:.12g}; actual calls {}.\n\n",coarse.solution.y.back()[0],std::exp(-4.),coarse.work.rhs_calls);
  std::print("| steps | h | endpoint error | error/h | previous error / current |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> logh,logerror,tx,yx,te,ye;double previous=0;
  for(int n=10;n<=2560;n*=2){const auto r=dm::algorithms::euler_method_work(f,{1},0,1,n);const auto h=r.step_size,error=std::exp(-1.)-r.solution.y.back()[0];std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.12g} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n",n,h,error,error/h,previous?previous/error:0);previous=error;logh.push_back(std::log10(h));logerror.push_back(std::log10(error));}
  std::print("\nPredicted limit error/h {:.12f}.\n",1/(2*std::exp(1.)));
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<coarse.solution.t.size();++i){te.push_back(coarse.solution.t[i]);ye.push_back(coarse.solution.y[i][0]);}for(int i=0;i<=200;++i){tx.push_back(4.*i/200);yx.push_back(std::exp(-tx.back()));}
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();a.line(tx,yx,"exact",{22,163,74}).line(te,ye,"Euler h=0.5",{37,99,235}).points(te,ye,"",{37,99,235},5).title("Original eight steps over [0,4]").x_label("t").y_label("State y");
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();b.line(logh,logerror,"actual error",{220,38,38}).points(logh,logerror,"",{220,38,38},5).title("Endpoint error approaches first order").x_label("log10(nominal h)").y_label("log10(endpoint absolute error)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,520).title("Original decay: coarse bias and measured refinement error").fit_panels().save_svg("build/euler_original.svg");
}
