> Input contracts and finite time grids

Euler requires a nonempty finite initial state, a nonempty callback,
finite endpoints and a positive integer step count. Every returned
derivative must have exactly the state dimension and contain only
finite values. Products h*f[i] and updated double states must remain
finite. Invalid shapes and arithmetic failures throw explicitly;
user callback exceptions propagate unchanged. A successful integration
can still have a large truncation error or an unstable solution.

Reverse integration uses negative h. Equal endpoints are supported:
h=0, N callbacks and N+1 repeated-time unchanged states are retained.
For a nonzero interval, the whole rounded time grid must advance
strictly before any callback is invoked. Nominal h is converted from
an extended-precision endpoint difference divided by N; interior times
use extended t0+i*h, and the last time is exactly the requested endpoint.
Adjacent rounded times need not differ by exactly h; all state updates
use the same nominal h. This policy avoids a hidden overflow in a
large endpoint subtraction, and rejects a step too small to advance
at the represented time scale. Refining indefinitely cannot bypass
floating-point resolution or state-update rounding.

Ordinary result/work calls return their intrinsic O((N+1)d) trajectory
and keep no optional derivative history or capture quota. Trace capture
counts each of N+1 sample slots with d state entries, and each of N
derivative slots with d derivative entries. Since C=(2N+1)(d+1) is
known upfront, the full quota is checked before history copies or any
callback. Default one million; zero disables that quota. Other outcome
metadata, runtime state/callback storage, vector headers, capacities
and allocator bytes are excluded. C is not a peak-memory estimate.
Returned samples and derivatives own their storage. Exceptions return
no partial result, although a later callback failure can follow earlier
callback side effects.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/euler_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");int calls=0;auto decay=[&](double,const std::vector<double>& y){++calls;return std::vector<double>{-y[0]};};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::euler_method_trace(decay,{1},0,1,2,10);calls=0;int rejections=0;
  for(std::size_t cap=1;cap<10;++cap)try{(void)dm::algorithms::euler_method_trace(decay,{1},0,1,2,cap);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejections;}
  const int quota_failure_calls=calls;const auto disabled=dm::algorithms::euler_method_trace(decay,{1},0,1,2,0);
  std::print("Capture: exact entries {}; insufficient quotas rejected {}/9; callbacks during quota failures {}; disabled-quota entries {}.\n",t.captured_entries,rejections,quota_failure_calls,disabled.captured_entries);
  const auto reverse=dm::algorithms::euler_method_work([](double,const std::vector<double>&){return std::vector<double>{2};},{1},2,-2,8);const auto zero=dm::algorithms::euler_method_work([](double,const std::vector<double>&){return std::vector<double>{2};},{1},2,2,3);
  std::print("Reverse interval: h {}; final time {}; final state {}. Zero interval: h {}; samples {}; actual callbacks {}; final state {}.\n",reverse.step_size,reverse.solution.t.back(),reverse.solution.y.back()[0],zero.step_size,zero.solution.t.size(),zero.work.rhs_calls,zero.solution.y.back()[0]);
  int domain=0;bool underflow=false,grid=false;const double nan=std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN();
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::euler_method_work(decay,{1},0,1,0);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++domain;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::euler_method_work([](double,const std::vector<double>&){return std::vector<double>{};},{1},0,1,1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++domain;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::euler_method_work([nan](double,const std::vector<double>&){return std::vector<double>{nan};},{1},0,1,1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++domain;}
  calls=0;try{(void)dm::algorithms::euler_method_work(decay,{1},0,std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min(),2);}catch(const std::underflow_error&){underflow=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::euler_method_work(decay,{1},1e100,std::nextafter(1e100,std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()),4);}catch(const std::runtime_error&){grid=true;}
  std::print("Controls: step-zero/wrong-shape/NaN-derivative rejected {}/3; step underflow {}; rounded-grid failure {}; calls during time failures {}.\n",domain,underflow,grid,calls);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4.1;box.height=1.6;box.font_size=23;
  d.node("samples",{0,0},std::format("{} sample slots\n{} state entries",t.outcome.solution.t.size(),t.outcome.solution.y.size()),box).node("derivatives",{5.5,0},std::format("{} derivative slots\n{} derivative entries",t.derivatives.size(),t.derivatives.size()),box)
   .edge("samples","derivatives").text({2.75,2},std::format("Logical capture: (3 + 2) * (1 + 1) = {}",t.captured_entries),26).text({2.75,-1.8},"Full quota checked before the first callback",22).save_svg("build/euler_limits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Capture: exact entries 10; insufficient quotas rejected 9/9; callbacks during quota failures 0; disabled-quota entries 10.
Reverse interval: h -0.5; final time -2; final state -7. Zero interval: h 0; samples 4; actual callbacks 3; final state 1.
Controls: step-zero/wrong-shape/NaN-derivative rejected 3/3; step underflow true; rounded-grid failure true; calls during time failures 0.
\image(figures/euler_limits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The complete ten-entry quota succeeds; all nine positive insufficient quotas fail before any callback. Reverse, zero-time and unrepresentable-grid cases have separate explicit outcomes.)
\alttext(A native two-box diagram separates three intrinsic sample slots and three state entries from two actual derivative slots and two derivative entries, totaling ten logical entries.)

The test suite verifies closed discrete scalar/vector solutions,
nonautonomous quadrature, oscillator evolution and energy, actual
stateful callback order, ownership, exact quotas, strict domains and
floating limits. All 132 frozen legacy state trajectories, totaling
16,268 samples, retain exactly the same state values and callback/sample
counts. Two time grids change by at most 1.4211e-14 through the documented
rounding policy; all other grids are exact matches. The old malformed
callback probe triggered an out-of-bounds access, and its nonpositive
step/NaN/rounded-grid probes were accepted; those unsafe cases now fail.
Explicit ODE oracles are the natural inputs for these checks, so no
external dataset or independent solver setup is required by the report.

> References

E. Hairer and C. Lubich, Numerical solution of ordinary differential
equations, sections 1.1 and the consistency/stability discussion,
https://www.unige.ch/~hairer/preprints/pcam-ode.pdf.

C. Moler, Numerical Computing with MATLAB, chapter 7, Ordinary
Differential Equations, PDF version dated September 17, 2013,
https://www.mathworks.com/content/dam/mathworks/mathworks-dot-com/moler/odes.pdf.

These sources describe explicit Euler and its accuracy/stability
framework. The algebraic decay and oscillator checks above are shown
explicitly; finite validation, grid rounding and bounded actual-history
capture are datamunge implementation choices.
